# Revision history for PAGI-Tools

0.003000 - 2026-09-30

  Most of the 0.002002 API is replaced; there are no compatibility aliases.
  UPGRADING.md has a table of what replaces what and a worked port;
  UPGRADING-REFERENCE.md has the details. Needs PAGI::Server 0.003000.

  [New]
  - PAGI::Tools::Extending: a guide for building frameworks and add-ons on
    PAGI-Tools -- what a framework may rely on, the two ways to build one,
    and a link to the rule for each; examples/framework-wrap-style and
    examples/framework-on-tools are the complete frameworks.
  - PAGI::Response exports status_forbids_body($status): true for the
    statuses that carry no content (1xx, 204, 205, 304), the rule every
    response class applies.
  - PAGI::App: an optional base class for applications, in
    Plack::Component's shape (new, prepare_app, to_app, call), whose
    run_if_script, the module's last line in place of 1;, makes
    lib/MyApp.pm both an application a server loads
    (pagi-server -Ilib --app lib/MyApp.pm) and a program you can run.
    Loading it builds nothing until a server calls to_app.
  - PAGI::App::Compose: the regular way to write a Compose application in
    lib/MyApp.pm. A subclass writes routes, and optionally app_middleware,
    http_default, on_startup and on_shutdown; to_app builds them once into
    a PAGI::Compose application, composition returns the description for
    tests, and writing call is refused. An application hook, given the
    composition, returns what to serve (openapi adds its document there).
  - PAGI::State lifetimes: singleton { }, per_request { } and factory { }
    values in application state, built on first get -- once per worker,
    once per request, or on every get -- by a builder that may be async (get
    then gives a Future, shared by every caller of a pending build; a failed
    or cancelled build is built again by the next get). PAGI::State::Deferred
    is the seam for other lifetimes.
  - PAGI::Routing route_table (Router and Compose): every route in the tree
    as a PAGI::Routing::RouteEntry -- its path template and declared
    pattern, kind, methods, name and absolute address, desc, capture names
    and constraints as declared, the middleware around it, its meta, and the
    node -- so a tool describes an application (an OpenAPI document, a route
    listing, a test of its surface) from the public API. A meta option on
    routes and mounts holds descriptive data for such tools; PAGI::Routing
    never reads it.
  - PAGI::Test::Response header_pairs: every response header as
    [name, value] pairs, in order, repeats (Set-Cookie) kept.
  - PAGI::Test::Client lifespan => 'auto': a server's default lifespan mode.
    An application that declines lifespan (raises on the lifespan scope, or
    returns without signalling startup) runs without it; only
    lifespan.startup.failed fails start. lifespan => 1 stays strict, and a
    decline after off-loop I/O now fails start with the application's own
    exception at once instead of the drive timeout. lifespan => 'on' is
    strict, and lifespan => 'off', a true string, is refused instead of
    turning strict lifespan on.
  - PAGI::Utils::TextDecoder: decodes a byte stream to text as it arrives
    (Encode's stateful decoder), strict or with U+FFFD; body_stream's decode
    is built on it.
  - PAGI::Utils::Middleware record_receive (also from PAGI::Tools): for
    middleware that reads the request body before the application runs, a
    receive to read through and one that delivers what was read again,
    then the client's receive (so the disconnect still arrives). The
    events are kept in memory, or in a buffer given with buffer: a
    PAGI::Utils::ReceiveBuffer::TempFile (64KB in memory, the rest in a
    temporary file) or any object with append and take. Its POD cites the
    Starlette write-ups of this read-ahead pattern and the mistakes their
    hand-written receives make; PAGI::Middleware's "Reading the request
    body" says when to use it and when to wrap the receive instead.
  - PAGI::ContentNegotiation's handler side: respond_to, negotiated_type
    and content_negotiation (also from PAGI::Tools) read the
    ContentNegotiation middleware's choice; respond_to calls the branch for
    the chosen type.
  - PAGI::Routing (route, websocket, sse, mount, router, path_for,
    url_for) and PAGI::Compose (application root with lifespan, error
    handling and a response guard).
  - A named mount is a path_for/url_for target: its prefix, or a path below
    it with path (path_for($request, 'static', { path => 'app.css' })). A
    route and a mount cannot share a name.
  - Response classes: Text, HTML, JSON, Problem, Redirect, Empty, File,
    Stream and NDJSON, built by name with response('Name', ...) -- resolved
    like middleware(), '+' for a Response class from another distribution.
  - Stream's format_item and Writer's write_item: a Stream subclass decides
    how each item is written (NDJSON is one), and on a plain Stream
    write_item is write.
  - PAGI::Pages: negotiated welcome, error and redirect responses.
  - PAGI::ErrorContext (error_context): the error an ErrorHandler handler
    answers. PAGI::Exception::RequestBody gains client_message.
  - PAGI::Auth (auth, auth_result, unauth_result, www_authenticate)
    and PAGI::Middleware::Authentication. Its backend reads the request's
    headers and cookies, never the body (as Starlette's backend gets an
    HTTPConnection); a request signed over its body is checked in the
    handler.
  - PAGI::Middleware::Requires: the scopes a route, mount or application
    requires, as middleware (Starlette's @requires). Otherwise a plain 403,
    a refuse application, or a redirect 303: to a location as written, or
    to path_for arguments with ?next= added. WebSocket and SSE are refused
    before they start, and route_table shows it in each entry's middleware.
  - PAGI::Routing::OpenAPI: openapi($app, title => ..., version => ...)
    returns the application, a Compose, with its OpenAPI 3.1 document as a
    route at /openapi.json, inside the application's own middleware. The
    document is built from route_table: paths and methods, typed path parameters,
    operationIds, summaries from desc, security from Requires, and the
    request and response bodies a route declares in meta. Mounted
    applications describe themselves. openapi_document returns the document
    alone; a subclass can override operation_id, parameter_schema,
    security_for or operation. PAGI::Tools re-exports openapi.
  - PAGI::State, PAGI::CSRF, PAGI::Transport and PAGI::Routing::URL
    helpers; PAGI::Authority; PAGI::Utils::Headers; PAGI::Utils
    path_from_root, pagi_env and environment predicates.
  - request_uri and raw_path_info on Request, WebSocket and SSE;
    PAGI::Exception::RequestBody (bad bodies answer 400, 413 or 415).
  - Request charset: the Content-Type charset parameter, to decode a
    streamed body by (body_stream(decode => $req->charset // 'UTF-8')).
  - body_stream with strict: bytes invalid in the encoding are a
    RequestBody 400 (they were a 500), a valid character split across chunks
    is no longer rejected, an encoding it cannot decode in chunks (unknown,
    ISO-2022-*, UTF-7) is a RequestBody 415 when the stream is made; decoding
    no longer holds back the rest of the body after a bad byte; UTF-16 and
    UTF-32 decode correctly across chunks, strict included; and a strict
    error is reported on the chunk that carries it.
  - Request text decodes with the declared charset, UTF-8 when none is
    declared; a charset Perl does not know is a RequestBody 415
    (unsupported_charset).
  - Session warns, once per process, when it falls back to the in-memory
    store because no store was given: those sessions are lost on restart
    and not shared between workers.
  - WebSocket and SSE connection, as Request has: the scope's
    pagi.connection, for the parts of the spec interface the helpers do
    not wrap.
  - Session's session_state and session_store constructors.
  - PAGI::Test::Client root_path; PAGI::Middleware::BufferedResponse;
    App::File from_app_path; the process-streaming example.
  - PAGI::WebSocket close_or_deny: ends a socket however its state allows
    -- deny before accept, close after, nothing once the client has gone.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF: session => 1 keeps the token in the session
    and replaces it when the session is regenerated (at login);
    an origin check (Sec-Fetch-Site, then Origin vs Host) on unsafe
    requests and on WebSocket handshakes (cross-site WebSocket hijacking),
    with trusted_origins; httponly. It never reads the body. With
    form_field set, a urlencoded or multipart post without the header is
    left to PAGI::CSRF's new csrf_protect, which wraps a form route's
    handler and reads the field from the handler's own Request (as
    starlette-wtf's csrf_protect does), so one token serves every form on
    a page and the handler's form_params is the same parse.
  - PAGI::FutureOwner: own the Futures you start until they settle; await
    settled for all of them (in lifespan.shutdown, say). Replaces ->retain
    for background work.
  - PAGI::WebSocket and PAGI::SSE: finished, which resolves once the
    scope has ended and the helper's own work (its close, best-effort sends
    and on_close callbacks) has settled.
  - Text and HTML responses declare the UTF-8 they always send: a custom
    content type without a charset gets "; charset=utf-8" (JSON types
    excepted), whether given to the constructor or set later.
  - PAGI::Exception::HTTP: throw an HTTP error with a status from 400 to
    599 and an optional client message --
    PAGI::Exception::HTTP->throw(404, 'no such note') -- and ErrorHandler
    answers with that status and message.
  - PAGI::Tools exports the everyday toolkit functions by name -- use
    PAGI::Tools qw(compose response route path_for) -- or by the :routes,
    :urls and :all tags. Each is its source module's own function, so
    importing from the modules themselves still works the same.
  - Reading the request body in middleware, documented as one rule --
    a body arrives once, so whoever reads it hands the application a
    receive that delivers a body -- in PAGI::Middleware's "Reading the
    request body", with its three shapes: wrap the receive to watch or
    change the body as it streams (Starlette's documented pattern; the
    Cookbook's new "Watching or changing the request body", a size logger
    and a gzip decoder); read ahead with record_receive to pass on the
    same body (buffer_request_body is the read half); or write the receive
    yourself to pass on a different one (examples/modify-form-post, a
    middleware that rewrites a form POST, beside the same application in
    Starlette). examples/signed-requests checks a signature over the body
    as the routes read it, as Starlette's body-size limit does.
  - PAGI::Utils exports request_for_scope($scope, $receive): a new Request
    of the application's class (RequestClass's, else PAGI::Request), for
    middleware, error handlers and a framework's own dispatch to read what
    the scope holds. A limited-use Request: not the route handler's, and
    it does not own the body; code that must not read the body passes a
    receive that dies, as Authentication and ErrorHandler do.
  - Cookbook: "Linking inside a mounted app"; PAGI::Routing::URL's
    path_for shows absolute, sibling and parent references in a Mount.
  - examples/gut-check: everyday applications next to their Starlette
    versions, with measured behaviour and line counts, tested in
    t/example-gut-check.t.
  - PAGI::Middleware::RequestClass: middleware('RequestClass', class =>
    'MyApp::Request') gives every handler below it (routes, Endpoint::HTTP
    methods, refuse, deny and decline handlers, an ErrorHandler catch
    entry, Authentication backends listed after it) that Request class instead
    of PAGI::Request, through the scope key pagi.request.factory;
    request_response's request_factory still wins for its one handler.
  - examples/request-class: an application's Request class set once by
    middleware.
  - PAGI::Endpoint and endpoint() (PAGI::Routing, PAGI::Tools):
    route('/x' => endpoint('Class', %args)) loads the class and builds a
    fresh object with new for each request (each connection for WebSocket
    and SSE), so $self belongs to that request. A BUILD_APP that returns an
    object shares it across every request; one that returns a coderef is a
    builder, called per request (it may be async); for HTTP the handler
    gets the Request the builder was given, so a Request class's new runs
    once per request. Moose endpoint classes need MooseX::NonMoose, and
    endpoint() says so.
  - Endpoint::HTTP answers QUERY with a query method. A query helper method
    on an existing subclass now answers QUERY too.
  - PAGI::Test::Client has a query method for the QUERY verb.
  - PAGI::Response->new with no arguments is the empty value: no explicit
    status, no Content-Type, an empty body; a framework stages status,
    headers and cookies on it before it knows the final class (as
    FastAPI's temporary Response). Undef content is an empty body in the
    base class (JSON still renders undef as null; Text and HTML still
    refuse it), and the base class writes its application/octet-stream
    only when the body has bytes, as Starlette's base Response has no
    media type: an empty untyped redirect sends no Content-Type.
  - A status that forbids content (1xx, 204, 205, 304) takes an empty body
    in the base class, Text and HTML, not only Empty:
    response('Text', '', status => 204) is a 204, sent with no default
    Content-Type and, on 1xx/204/304, no Content-Length. A body with bytes
    on such a status is still refused, and Stream, NDJSON and File still
    refuse the status; Empty remains the class that never carries a
    Content-Type.
  - PAGI::Exception::HTTP takes headers (new(401, $message, headers =>
    ['WWW-Authenticate' => ...])), and ErrorHandler's built-in answer sends
    an HTTP error's headers, from any thrown object with a headers method:
    a 401, 405, 407 or 426 that carries its required field no longer needs
    a catch entry. Headers that cannot be sent make the built-in answer a
    500 with a diagnostic; a catch entry answers in full, with the claimed
    status whatever the headers are, and sends them only if it passes them
    on -- except a 405's Allow, which an entry's 405 that sends none gets
    from the error, wherever the ErrorHandler is placed.
  - ErrorHandler catch => { 404 => sub ($request, $error) {...},
    'MyApp::Conflict' => sub {...} }: the errors the application expects,
    answered by status and exception class in Starlette's order (an HTTP
    error's status, then the class and its ancestors, then the status a
    plain die was given).
  - ErrorHandler is two boundaries, as Starlette's ExceptionMiddleware and
    ServerErrorMiddleware are. The exception boundary handles an expected
    error -- an HTTP error, a thrown page, or one with a catch entry -- and
    a handled error is not re-raised, whatever its status; the
    server-error boundary answers the rest with handler (or a catch entry
    for the configured status, or the built-in 500) and always re-raises.
    An error a route's handler throws -- a coderef route, an Endpoint::HTTP
    method, a builder that takes a Request -- is answered at that route,
    so a catch entry gets the route's own Request: the form it read, its
    path_params, the session and CSRF keys (as Starlette's request_response
    hands an exception handler the endpoint's Request). In Compose's
    middleware the stock ErrorHandler is split, wherever it is listed:
    catch around the router, inside every application middleware; handler,
    on_error and the 500 outside them all, so an application middleware's
    error gets handler, never catch, and request identity, access logging
    and response headers that must cover the 500 wrap the compiled
    application. One per Compose; a subclass is not split. The innermost
    ErrorHandler answers, as Starlette's innermost ExceptionMiddleware
    does: an outer catch table never reaches past an inner ErrorHandler or
    into a mounted Compose. When that innermost one has catch entries, the
    router raises its 404 and 405 (with Allow) to it, as Starlette's does;
    with no entry for them they get the router's own plain answers. ErrorHandler's answers to HEAD send no body. A failing
    handler or entry fails as itself, outward, with one warning naming the
    error it displaced. Cookbook: "Re-render a form from an error handler";
    examples/internal-tool raises its board's errors and answers them in
    one place, beside the same board in Flask.
  - A Stream's content may be a source, any object with next_chunk:
    response('Stream', $source) pulls it one value at a time, as
    Starlette's StreamingResponse iterates its content (NDJSON takes a
    source of items). Stream calls nothing else on a source; the on_close
    option is its cleanup, for a source or a producer.

  [Changed]
  - Tests: the two that would meet Future::XS's "lost a sequence Future"
    warning skip under a Future::XS older than 0.16, which fixes it
    (RT #181389); PAGI-Server's --future-xs requires 0.16.
  - PAGI::Test::Client: an SSE application that returns after sse.start
    without sse.close ends with disconnect_detail "sse.start without
    sse.close", as PAGI::Server records it (it was undef).
  - One headers argument everywhere: the response options (every class),
    PAGI::Pages, PAGI::WebSocket's accept and PAGI::SSE's start take a flat
    [name => value] list, [[name, value]] pairs as the PAGI spec writes
    headers, or a PAGI::Headers object (copied). Any other shape croaks
    with one message naming the site. accept and start now send pairs (a
    flat list or an object reached the server in the wrong shape) and check
    each value by the header value rule. PAGI::Test::Client is unchanged.
  - Header names are HTTP tokens (RFC 9110) wherever Tools sends one:
    header(), every headers option, accept, start, and a name added
    straight to a response's PAGI::Headers (checked at send). A name with a
    space, a colon or a character above 0x7E croaks, naming it; before,
    accept sent an empty name as a nameless line and a wide name killed
    PAGI::Server mid-handshake. Pages already had this rule.
  - Byte APIs (PAGI::Response->new, a Stream writer's write, an explicit
    File etag, PAGI::WebSocket's send_bytes and try_send_bytes) take any
    string whose characters are all at most 0xFF, whatever Perl's internal
    UTF-8 flag: ASCII from Encode::decode, or built with a decoded string,
    was refused as "not encoded bytes" (#57), and send_bytes did not check
    at all. Header values (header(), the headers option, content_type(), and
    every header as it is sent) and cookie parts follow the same rule; a wide
    character there used to pass Tools, or die inside Cookie::Baker. A
    character above 0xFF croaks, naming the fix. Decoded text
    through a byte API is sent as ISO-8859-1; send text with Text or HTML.
    See BYTES AND CHARACTERS in PAGI::Response.
  - Wherever a response takes a string it also takes an object whose class
    overloads "" (URI, Path::Tiny, DateTime, exception objects), stringified
    once; every other value that is not a string croaks, including objects
    that overload only numeric or boolean conversion. 0.002's Text and HTML
    handed any value to Encode, so a reference went out as HASH(0x...) and
    undef as an empty string: a side effect, not a feature, and it is not
    kept. Applies to Text, HTML and byte bodies; Stream write and
    write_text; the Redirect location; Problem type, instance, title and
    detail (the document passed in is not changed); and PAGI::Pages' detail,
    title, type, instance, redirect targets, blocked_by, login_url,
    favicon_href, header values, cache_control, challenge and retry_after;
    File's path and filename; and header values, the
    Content-Type and a cookie's value, domain and path. A cookie part that
    is not a string, an undef value included, now croaks instead of going
    out as HASH(0x...) or "c="; and every header a response sends (headers
    added through $response->headers too) is checked as it is sent, naming
    a header whose value is not a string. PAGI::WebSocket's send_text,
    send_bytes and close reason, and PAGI::SSE's send, send_comment and
    send_event (data, event, id), with their try_send variants, follow the
    same rule; send_event's id no longer goes out as HASH(0x...) for an
    object, and undef data is refused by both send_event and
    try_send_event (it went to the server, which refused it, or out as an
    empty event).
  - content_type(undef) gives back the class's default type instead of
    removing Content-Type, so a Text, HTML, JSON or other typed response
    always says what it is; Empty and File, which have no default, send none.
  - PAGI::WebSocket's try_send_text, try_send_bytes and try_send_json report
    a failure to on_error, as PAGI::SSE's try_send* already did; they still
    return false and never throw.
  - One way to load a class given by name, shared by middleware(),
    Builder's enable, response(), session_store/session_state, endpoint()
    and RequestClass: a class already defined (declared in app.pl, or
    loaded) is used as it is, so an inline class needs no $INC line;
    otherwise its file is loaded, and a missing or failing file dies
    "NAME('...'): cannot load CLASS: ...". A class whose file died while
    loading is never used half-defined. endpoint()'s message was
    "endpoint class X could not be loaded"; RequestClass's gains a colon.
  - Request is_json is true for every +json media type
    (application/vnd.api+json, application/problem+json, ...), not only
    application/json.
  - PAGI::Exception::RequestBody documents unsupported_media_type (415) for a body in a media
    type the route does not take; the Cookbook's "Accept JSON and a form on
    one route" shows the one way to read either and refuse the rest.
  - Encode 3.00 or later is required (perl 5.30 and later ship it).
  - ContentNegotiation sends Vary: Accept on every answer, its 406 and
    refusals included, merged into any Vary the response has.
  - raw_path is the full requested path at every mount level; AccessLog
    logs, and HTTPSRedirect redirects to, the encoded request URI.
  - Stock error responses from first-party components are plain text, each
    replaceable by one option (refuse, response, http_default, default, or
    ErrorHandler's catch and handler); a PAGI::Pages page is the
    application's choice.
  - One coderef convention: a slot that answers a request (every refuse,
    Maintenance response, ErrorHandler handler, routes, http_default,
    deny/decline) reads a bare coderef as a Request handler; a slot that
    hosts an application (mount, URLMap, Cascade, Builder) reads it as a
    native app.
  - ErrorHandler answers in plain text (reason phrase, or a client error's
    client_message) with Cache-Control: no-store; its handler is a Request
    handler that reads the error through PAGI::ErrorContext
    (error_context($request)), answers only unhandled errors, may return any
    application, and declines by returning $error->default. Status claims
    outside 400-599 answer 500.
  - References in events are shared, not copied (PAGI 0.002013): Runtime,
    SecurityHeaders and CORS no longer write into a response's own header
    list, nor Debug into its header pairs, which leaked one request's headers -- an origin, a timing
    -- into later requests when an application reused that list. New
    PAGI::Utils::Middleware::wrap_response_headers hands middleware a
    PAGI::Headers copy to edit. When the response already carries the
    header: Runtime leaves it and warns, SecurityHeaders leaves it, CORS
    replaces it (each sent a second value before). CORS and
    SecurityHeaders now also add their headers when the application sent
    none.
  - PAGI-Tools middleware reject unknown options: a misspelt or renamed
    option (CORS `origin`, CSRF `invalid`) dies at construction.
  - The Router's stock HTTP default is 0.002002's plain-text 404 "Not
    Found"; http_default stays the override.
  - URLMap answers an unmatched request with 0.002002's plain-text 404
    again. Builder's mount now is URLMap, so mount '/' takes every path,
    as in Plack, rather than only /; URLMap's mount('api') now mounts
    /api, as Builder's did.
  - Maintenance answers with a plain-text 503 by default; its response
    option (any application) replaces the body and content_type options,
    which now die.
  - App::File (and Directory and Static through it) refuses with 0.002002's
    plain text, keeping the 405's Allow and the 416's Content-Range; its
    refuse option replaces all four refusals, with the reason in
    pagi.file_failure -- a branded PAGI::Pages page is one choice.
  - The Router's and Endpoint::HTTP's 405 is plain text with Allow, as in
    0.002002; an endpoint class answers it differently by overriding
    method_not_allowed, and the endpoint still sets Allow.
  - CSRF, TrustedHosts, HTTPSRedirect, ReverseProxy, RateLimit and
    ContentNegotiation refuse with plain text (CSRF a 403, RateLimit a 429,
    ContentNegotiation a 406 listing the supported types, the others a
    400); their refuse option replaces the refusal with any application. RateLimit
    still sets Retry-After and X-RateLimit-* on whatever refuses, keeps
    each instance's buckets apart, counts time below a second, and calls
    itself a proof of concept in its POD. CSRF's refuse => 0 lets the application decide, and
    csrf($request)->valid and ->failure report its check.
  - GZip compresses streaming responses.
  - Cascade advances only on a status in catch; a child that sends
    nothing is an error, not a miss.
  - Host data is validated strictly: Request authority, TrustedHosts and
    HTTPSRedirect answer 400 for malformed or duplicate Host; ReverseProxy
    accepts one trusted X-Forwarded-Host.
  - Accept matching uses the most specific quality (an exact q=0 wins).
  - XSendfile mappings match path components, most specific first.
  - The Session middleware re-sends the cookie on a read once half of
    expire has passed.
  - PAGI::WebSocket and PAGI::SSE send one message at a time.
  - A routed or Endpoint WebSocket/SSE call now ends with its scope --
    after the closing handshake -- and its on_close cleanup, so a
    shutting-down PAGI::Server waits for that cleanup. Every
    on_close callback runs; the first failure fails the call (the server
    logs it) instead of being warned.
  - on_close registered after the connection has ended runs at once,
    after any callbacks still running, so cleanup registered once its
    resource exists is never missed, whenever the client leaves. A late
    callback's failure fails finished, or is warned once finished has
    resolved.
  - PAGI::App::WrapCGI: a refused request completes once its script has
    been reaped.
  - Nothing in PAGI-Tools uses ->retain; the cookbook and the
    background-tasks example teach PAGI::FutureOwner instead.
  - PAGI::Test::Client: a WebSocket or SSE application that fails after
    accepting or starting -- an on_close failure included -- fails the
    test, as an HTTP application's does; it used to pass silently.
  - PAGI::Middleware::Lint no longer notes an SSE stream the client left
    (client_closed, a closed tab): a client cannot announce that it is
    leaving, so that is the stream's ordinary end. Other abnormal ends,
    and WebSocket and HTTP disconnects, are still noted.
  - ErrorHandler answers a thrown page -- a value with to_app and no
    status_code, such as a Response -- with the page itself. Below 400 it
    is sent as it is, with no on_error, handler or re-raise, so
    die response('Redirect', '/login') redirects; from 400 to 599 it is a
    handled error: on_error runs and the page is the answer, not re-raised.
    A thrown Response used to be a 500.
  - $request->state, $ws->state and $sse->state always return a
    PAGI::State. Without application state it is empty, and its get and
    data die naming the likely cause (lifespan startup did not run;
    PAGI::Test::Client needs lifespan => 1). app_state and
    PAGI::State->new still return undef there.
  - Every module's tag for all its exports is :all. Compose, CSRF,
    Transport, Stash, Session, ErrorContext, Routing, State and
    Routing::URL used :ALL, which no release shipped.
  - Every bundled middleware's SYNOPSIS leads with Compose's
    middleware('Name', ...), then shows PAGI::Middleware::Name->new(...)
    ->wrap($app); PAGI::Middleware::Builder is documented as the way to
    compose middleware without Compose.
  - The Tutorial's middleware part leads with Compose's middleware list
    and wrap; Builder is a short section for applications without Compose.
  - The background-tasks example shows work started at once next to work
    started after the response (the connection's on_complete), and no
    longer uses IO::Async; a Cookbook recipe, "Work After the Response",
    explains the choice.
  - PAGI::SSE and PAGI::WebSocket document handlers and disconnects:
    strict sends for messages that must arrive, try_send_* for setup steps
    and broadcasts, start, accept and keepalive safe once the client has
    gone. The sse-dashboard example drops its is_closed checks.
  - PAGI::Routing's POD leads with the everyday API and keeps the complete
    rules in a REFERENCE part; component modules, the Cookbook and the
    Tutorial link to one statement of each rule instead of restating it.

  [Breaking -- see UPGRADING.md]
  - PAGI::Middleware::ErrorHandler: a handler's or catch entry's Request has
    no body to read; body, form_params, json and the rest die at once,
    naming the handler's line (as Starlette's ServerErrorMiddleware hands
    its handler a Request with no receive). The application may have read
    the body before it failed, and reading it again waited forever on a
    real server.
  - PAGI::Request writes nothing to the scope: the headers, query,
    cookies, body, form and uploads it parses, and its body latches, are
    kept on the object, as Starlette's Request keeps them (0.002002 kept
    them in the scope under pagi.request.*). A middleware that rewrites
    headers, the query string or cookies on a copy of the scope no longer
    leaves a stale parse for the Request below it. Two Requests over one
    scope each parse for themselves, and the second cannot read a body the
    first one read: pass the one Request on. PAGI-Tools' own middleware
    never reads the body (CSRF, MethodOverride and Authentication: see
    their entries), so a route below them may stream it.
  - Requires Perl 5.24 (was 5.18), as PAGI 0.003001 and PAGI-Server do.
    See "Perl version support" in PAGI.
  - PAGI::Test::Client no longer runs an application's own event loop
    for an HTTP request: 0.002002's ->get on the app's Future ran an
    IO::Async loop the app waited on, for requests only. An application
    that waits on a loop of its own binds Future::IO to it in the test
    (use Future::IO::Impl::IOAsync), and then requests, WebSocket and SSE
    waits and lifespan all run it. While Future::IO is bound to no event
    loop, a test-client timeout says so and points to the new POD section
    TESTING AN APPLICATION THAT COMMITS TO AN EVENT LOOP.
  - body_stream over max_bytes is a RequestBody 413 too_large (it was a plain
    die, so a 500), or 400 invalid_body past the request's own
    Content-Length; the message no longer names the limit, which moves to
    the error's cause.
  - body_stream with decode: a client disconnect no longer flushes a
    partial character as U+FFFD or throws under strict; truncated reports
    it.
  - ContentNegotiation writes one scope record, pagi.content_negotiation,
    in place of pagi.preferred_content_type and pagi.accepted_types; an
    unknown or empty supported_types dies; the stock 406 lists media types.
  - PAGI::Request::Negotiate is now PAGI::ContentNegotiation (the same
    class methods), with is_known_type and shortcuts.
  - PAGI::App::Router and PAGI::Endpoint::Router are removed; PAGI::Routing
    is the routing API.
  - The PAGI::Context family is removed: handlers receive PAGI::Request,
    PAGI::WebSocket or PAGI::SSE and return a response value.
  - The mutable PAGI::Response builder and $request->response are replaced
    by complete Response classes (response('JSON', ...), ...); respond
    and is_response are gone.
  - PAGI::App::NotFound and PAGI::App::Redirect are replaced by
    PAGI::Pages; ErrorHandler loses content_type.
  - PAGI::App::Loader, PAGI::Middleware::FormBody and ::JSONBody,
    ::Auth::Basic and ::Auth::Bearer, ::Session::State::Bearer,
    ::WebSocket::RateLimit, PAGI::App::SSE::Pubsub and
    ::WebSocket::Broadcast/::Chat are removed.
  - Session takes no secret, cookie_name or cookie_options: the cookie is
    configured on State::Cookie, which sets no Max-Age by default and
    merges its cookie_options into the defaults.
  - ErrorHandler re-raises an unhandled error to the server after the error
    response (an HTTP error or a thrown page is handled, whatever its
    status), and rethrows an exception raised after the response started
    instead of swallowing it. In Compose its 500 is rendered outside the
    application middleware: RequestId, AccessLog, CORS and SecurityHeaders
    that must cover it wrap the compiled application.
  - PAGI::WebSocket and PAGI::SSE require pagi.connection (PAGI::Server
    0.003000); their state is a PAGI::State object; deny takes a response.
  - A request body cut short by a disconnect croaks instead of returning
    the bytes that arrived.
  - File, Directory and Static share one request-path contract: hidden
    files forbidden by default (Directory show_hidden becomes allow_hidden),
    configured symlinks served, Directory 404/405 where it said 403/200.
  - Middleware Builder's exact-package prefix is + instead of ^.
  - The test kit is as strict as PAGI::Server: illegal sends fail, a
    missing terminal event is a server_error, an app that never responds
    gets a 500, and each terminal disconnect is delivered once.
  - Lint's strict mode rejects an illegal send instead of forwarding it.
  - App::WebSocket::Echo's on_disconnect receives ($scope, $code, $reason).
  - PAGI::WebSocket and PAGI::SSE on_close callbacks receive a third
    argument, $detail; a callback with a strict two-argument signature
    dies, and its cleanup is skipped (the error is logged).
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's enforce is removed: refuse => 0 replaces
    enforce => 'app', and passing enforce dies.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF accepts a plain HTML form only with
    form_field => 'csrf_token' and csrf_protect around each form route's
    handler (0.002002's enforce => 'app' with the handler's verify);
    without form_field, an unsafe request without the X-CSRF-Token header
    is refused. It never reads the body, so a route that streams an upload
    checks the token from the stream itself (see the Cookbook).
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's secret is removed (tokens are random; passing
    it dies); its cookie is no longer HttpOnly unless httponly => 1;
    cross-origin unsafe requests are refused. PAGI::CSRF's verify fails a
    cross-origin request.
  - PAGI::Middleware::RateLimit's backend is removed (it was never used);
    passing it dies.
  - PAGI::App::Throttle is removed; PAGI::Middleware::RateLimit is the one
    rate limiter.
  - PAGI::App::Proxy is removed: its blocking socket I/O froze the event
    loop for every proxied request.
  - PAGI::App::WrapCGI is rewritten on Future::IO. It no longer blocks the
    event loop, streams the script's output, passes it the request body
    (it never did), enforces its timeout (it never did), stops the script
    when the client goes away, and builds the environment per RFC 3875 --
    never HTTP_PROXY (httpoxy, CVE-2016-5385). Unix only.
  - ContentNegotiation refuses an unmatched request by default; strict and
    default_type are removed (refuse => 0 replaces strict => 0); passing
    either dies.
  - Middleware scope keys move under pagi.*, which PAGI::Spec::Www reserves
    for extensions: pagi.csrf_token, pagi.original_client,
    pagi.original_method, pagi.original_path, pagi.request_id.
  - PAGI::WebSocket's query and raw_query are now query_param and
    raw_query_param, the names PAGI::Request and PAGI::SSE use; all three
    have query_param, query_params, raw_query_param and raw_query_params.
    PAGI::Request's deprecated query, raw_query, form and raw_form are
    removed: use query_param, raw_query_param, form_params and
    raw_form_params.
  - PAGI::WebSocket's send_text_if_connected, send_bytes_if_connected and
    send_json_if_connected are removed. try_send_text, try_send_bytes and
    try_send_json send only while the socket is connected: before accept,
    too, they now return false and send nothing.
  - PAGI::WebSocket each_json closes with 1007 "invalid JSON" and returns
    when a message is not JSON; it used to die, a server error (1011). Its
    on_invalid_json option, or a subclass's handle_invalid_json, answers
    otherwise. The loop also ends once the socket is no longer connected,
    so nothing the peer sends after our Close reaches the callback.
    receive_json still dies on invalid JSON.
  - Endpoint::HTTP, ::WebSocket and ::SSE to_app is an instance method: on a
    class name it dies, pointing to endpoint('Class') or Class->new->to_app.
    It used to build the class with new and no arguments, ignoring BUILD_APP.

  [Fixed]
  - PAGI::Test::WebSocket: a receive the application cancels while it
    waits (Future->wait_any cancels the receive that loses a race) no longer
    swallows the next message the test sends; the next receive gets it, and
    a second pending receive is unaffected, as on a server.
  - A JSON response accepts any JSON value on every JSON::MaybeXS
    backend: response('JSON', undef) sends null and a string or number
    is sent as itself. On a backend that does not enable allow_nonref by
    default, they croaked "hash- or arrayref expected".
  - response('PAGI::Response', ...) builds the base class, and each
    loader's namespace root resolves to itself (it became
    PAGI::Response::PAGI::Response). A short name with :: that has no file
    suggests the leading + instead of listing @INC.
  - The byte-body refusal says "must be a defined string of encoded bytes,
    or an object that stringifies to one"; it said "unblessed scalar",
    though objects that stringify are accepted.
  - PAGI::Test::Client records the disconnect detail PAGI::Server records:
    an SSE app's failure as its first line, without " at FILE line N"; an
    SSE app that returns without starting, "no response was started"; a
    refusal left unfinished, "refusal started but never completed"; a
    WebSocket app that returns without accepting or refusing, "no response
    was started"; a WebSocket app that dies, at any stage (an unfinished
    refusal included, on SSE too), its first line, as PAGI-Server records
    it from its failure-path fixes; a
    WebSocket app that returns from an accepted socket without a closing
    handshake, "accepted socket left without a closing handshake". It
    recorded the whole exception, or no detail.
  - A Content-Type given in a response's headers option, or set with
    header('Content-Type', ...), goes through the class's content_type
    rule like the content_type option: Text and HTML keep their charset
    (response('Text', $s, headers => ['Content-Type' => 'text/csv']) sent
    text/csv with no charset), Empty refuses one, and header() replaces
    the type rather than adding a second.
  - PAGI::Middleware::MethodOverride's documentation said it read a
    _method field in a POST body; it reads only the header and the query
    string, as Plack::Middleware::MethodOverride does, and never the body,
    and the documentation now says so: an HTML form puts the method in its
    action URL (action="/tasks/1?_method=DELETE").
  - PAGI::Middleware::Builder's enable no longer ignores a middleware
    that fails to load: a file that did not compile, or died while
    loading, was used half-defined, and a missing one failed later at
    new (the error showed only under PAGI_DEBUG). It dies at enable.
  - PAGI::Test::Client drives the event loop while a request waits on real
    I/O: a streamed response whose producer awaits Future::IO now completes
    in-process instead of failing with a 500 (it previously worked only by
    accident, for handlers whose first wait was the I/O itself). A request
    still pending after 5 seconds dies with a timeout message.
  - PAGI::Test::WebSocket's receive_text, receive_bytes and receive_json,
    and PAGI::Test::SSE's receive_event and receive_json, wait up to their
    timeout, driving the event loop, for a message the application sends
    after real I/O. They used to throw at once.
  - PAGI::Test::Client's websocket and sse wait up to 5 seconds, driving the
    event loop, for a handler that awaits real I/O before accept or start,
    then die naming the timeout.
  - PAGI::Middleware::XSendfile removes Content-Length when it hands a
    file to the proxy, since the body it sends is empty (#19).
  - PAGI::Utils::Random's secure_random_bytes croaks unless its length is
    a non-negative integer; a bad length used to return an empty string
    with warnings, or die with an unrelated message (#23).
  - The chat example's server issues every new session id from secure
    random bytes and no longer adopts an id the client chose; ids used to
    come from the time and rand(), and the browser made up its own. Other
    users now see a separate public user id: the session id, which resumes
    a session, was listed in every room's users (#24).
  - PAGI::Middleware::ETag derives the ETag from the body's SHA-256 digest
    instead of MD5, so a crafted body cannot collide with another's
    validator; existing ETags change once (#22).
  - PAGI::Utils::Random takes its bytes from Crypt::URandom, now a
    required dependency, which uses a system call such as getrandom(2)
    where one exists; it used to open /dev/urandom on every call (#17).
  - GZip sent every compressed response as 200, whatever its status.
  - ETag, GZip and ContentLength corrupted file/fh bodies, worked from
    partial bodies, emitted a start without a status when the application
    sent none, and transformed range responses.
  - ContentLength wrote its length into the application's own headers
    array, so a reused array carried the first response's length.
  - ConditionalGet and HEAD failed on routes that declare trailers.
  - PAGI::SSE every() cancelled the live receive on almost every tick.
  - Endpoint::SSE keepalive failed against validating servers, and a
    declined Endpoint::SSE still sent sse.start.
  - The Response Writer and Stream skipped on_close cleanup on failure.
  - RequestId added its header to the application's own header list, so a
    reused list accumulated one ID per request.
  - AccessLog let a %0D%0A in the path forge log lines.
  - WrapPSGI set no REQUEST_URI and passed characters, not bytes.
  - PAGI::Lifespan re-ran startup on an out-of-phase message.
  - try_send_* with a dropped Future warned and needed ->retain; SSE
    could issue sse.start twice.
  - PAGI::Test::Client shared one lifespan state across requests, sent no
    raw_path, and lost notifications and errors after a terminal send.
  - PAGI::WebSocket each_text and each_bytes stopped at a message that is
    false in Perl ("0" or empty), as if the client had gone.
  - PAGI::Test::WebSocket in manual close mode handed the application a
    peer's Close before the transport completed; it now arrives when the
    scope ends, as PAGI::Server delivers it.

0.002002 - 2026-07-15
  - PAGI::Middleware::Session: non-'http' scopes that carry a headers key
    (WebSocket, SSE upgrades) now get a READ-ONLY session populated the same
    way 'http' does, instead of being skipped; nothing is ever saved for them,
    and an unresolvable id yields an empty hashref rather than a missing key.
    See the new "NON-HTTP SCOPES" POD section.
  - PAGI::App::Router: an unmatched WebSocket route now uses the optional
    websocket.http.response denial extension only when advertised; otherwise
    it closes before acceptance so the server supplies the bare 403 response.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF: new 'enforce' config -- 'header' (default,
    unchanged: unsafe methods need a matching header token or a 403) or 'app'
    (issue-only: mint/stash the token for every method, never auto-reject, and
    let the app validate). 'app' exists because the middleware cannot read
    form params without buffering the body stream.
  - The former Context facade gained csrf_token and csrf_verify (timing-safe
    token checks). Both inherited into PAGI::Nano contexts.
  - REMOVED PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's 'token_param' config: it was dead (the
    form-field lookup was never implemented). Use enforce => 'app' with the
    facade's csrf_verify method for form tokens.
  - PAGI::Utils::SecureCompare: new module for the constant-time string compare
    shared by PAGI::Middleware::CSRF and the former facade.
  - Docs: PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's USAGE section no longer contradicts itself
    (a JS getCookie() against an HttpOnly cookie); it now documents the header
    and form flows separately.
  - t/integration/sse-decline-end-to-end.t now skips unless PAGI::Server is
    >= 0.002005, the release that added the sse.http.response.* decline
    protocol it exercises. Fixes a CPAN Testers FAIL against 0.002001 (older
    servers answer an unmatched SSE route with 500 instead of 404).
  - PAGI::Middleware::Auth::Bearer: _secure_compare now rejects undef arguments
    instead of numifying them, so two undef (or undef vs empty) signatures no
    longer compare equal.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF: new 'secure' config (default 0) to set the Secure
    attribute on the CSRF cookie; enable for HTTPS deployments.
  - PAGI::WebSocket / PAGI::SSE: a per-message callback that dies in the
    iteration helpers (each_message/each_text/each_bytes/each_json, SSE each()
    and every()) now runs on_close cleanup before re-raising, instead of
    bypassing it. WebSocket::run() is unchanged -- it isolates errors and keeps
    serving.
  - The former protocol-specific Context wrappers now fire the underlying
    object's on_close when a terminal disconnect is driven through their run
    method (previously it only recorded the reason, so on_close never fired and
    is_closed/is_connected lied). Both also gained on_close() stubs pointing at
    $c->websocket->on_close / $c->sse->on_close.
  - PAGI::Test::Client: start() now drives the event loop during lifespan
    startup and dies on failure, instead of busy-waiting a 5s deadline without
    advancing the loop (which hung off-loop startup hooks and ignored
    lifespan.startup.failed).

0.002001 - 2026-06-30
  - PAGI::Endpoint / PAGI::Endpoint::Router: route-level event middleware is now
    spec-pure factory form, composed at build time. The vestigial call() and the
    old 4-arg ($scope, $receive, $send, $next) middleware form are removed; the
    endpoint owns the value-flow chain and responds at the framework boundary
    (await $next->() returns the handler's response value). WS/SSE route-level
    middleware now fail loudly (unsupported), and a value-flow non-response error
    names the offending method.
  - PAGI::SSE: close() now sends an explicit sse.close event (with an optional
    server-side reason) and wakes a parked run(); close() is async so on_close
    callbacks run to completion.
  - PAGI::Endpoint::SSE: await keepalive() so the sse.keepalive event is actually
    emitted on a fully-async server.
  - The endpoint and router dispatch paths formerly shared one nominal
    Response predicate so their diagnostics stayed consistent. The current
    application-valued contract supersedes that older release behavior.
  - PAGI::SSE and its former facade gained is_connected, derived from the SSE
    wrapper's own state machine (true while started and not closed), mirroring
    PAGI::WebSocket. The facade overrode the base method, which
    reads pagi.connection (not applicable to sse) and therefore wrongly reported
    a live stream as disconnected. Like the WebSocket counterpart it is "not
    falsely stale" rather than a live network probe.
  - PAGI::WebSocket->deny() no longer records the HTTP denial status as a
    WebSocket close code; close_code is undef after an HTTP denial (a denial
    sends a response, not a close frame). The bare-403 fallback still reports
    1008.
  - PAGI::WebSocket try_send_text/bytes/json no longer fabricate a 1006
    "Connection lost" close (and no longer mark the socket closed) when a send
    errors. A failed send returns false without mutating connection state; a
    real disconnect is still detected via the websocket.disconnect event.
  - Docs: PAGI::SSE scope caching described accurately (cached while
    referenced via a weak ref, not a guaranteed singleton);
    PAGI::Request::Negotiate content-negotiation example reads the Accept
    header through PAGI::Request instead of the (fatal) $scope->{headers}{...};
    try_send_* documented as best-effort with a send-method ladder; a cookbook
    recipe on declining an SSE request (404 / 401 / 204 stop-reconnect);
    examples and Endpoint POD migrated off the removed 4-arg middleware form.
  - REMOVED PAGI::Middleware::WebSocket::Compression. permessage-deflate
    (RFC 7692) is a WebSocket framing-layer feature -- a compressed message is
    signalled by the RSV1 frame bit, which only the server's framing layer can
    set. A middleware at the PAGI event layer can deflate the payload bytes but
    cannot flag the frame, so the deflated bytes went out in a normal RSV1=0
    frame (garbage on the wire). It also wrote a non-spec `extensions` key on
    websocket.accept (the event allows only `subprotocol`/`headers`), which the
    reference server ignores -- and that server explicitly does not implement
    permessage-deflate (RSV bits forced to 0). If WebSocket compression is
    wanted, it must be a server feature, not an application middleware.
  - PAGI::App::Router: an unmatched SSE or WebSocket route now returns a real
    404 instead of crashing the connection. The 404 is emitted with the event
    family that matches the scope -- sse.http.response.* on SSE and
    websocket.http.response.* on WebSocket, plain http.response.* on HTTP --
    since those scopes reject a plain http.response.* before a stream or
    handshake starts. A custom not_found app still takes over for every scope
    type. Requires a server supporting the sse.http.response.* decline events
    (PAGI-Server 0.002005+).

0.002000 - 2026-06-26
  - Split out of the PAGI distribution into its own distribution (git history
    preserved).
  - New PAGI::Tools module anchors the distribution.
  - The application runner ships in PAGI-Server as PAGI::Server::Runner.
  - B<BREAKING>: PAGI::Response is now a value — factories build detached
    responses, body methods return $self, respond($send) sends, to_app makes any
    response mountable; Endpoint HTTP handlers RETURN a response value
    (await $res->json(...) no longer sends).
  - B<BREAKING>: PAGI::Request->configure/->config removed; path_param strict and
    multipart limits are now per-call options.
  - PAGI::Headers: ordered, case-insensitive, multi-value header container
    (rejects undef values; to_hash); Response and Request route headers through it.
  - is_sent now reads response_started off the pagi.connection object, not a scope
    scalar, so it survives middleware's scope copy. Adds PAGI::Test::ConnectionState.
  - PAGI::Response->has_body_source: predicate for whether a body source was
    registered.
  - PAGI::Response: charset follows the body — text/html/send gain
    "; charset=utf-8", json() stays bare application/json.
  - PAGI::Utils::to_app coerces coderefs, app objects, and class names; every
    composition point accepts anything it accepts.
  - builder's enable() accepts configured middleware instances.
  - PAGI::App::Router: coderef route middleware can transform the channel.
  - PAGI::Endpoint::Router route middleware are value-flow (await $next->() returns
    the handler's response); App::Router dispatch returns the matched route's value.
  - The former Context facade gained text/html/json/redirect shorthands,
    assert_http/websocket/sse guards, on_default catch-all, raw_send accessor.
  - Backpressure/lifecycle helpers: on_complete, buffered_amount/high_water_mark/
    low_water_mark, on_high_water/on_drain/is_writable, WebSocket->deny().
  - PAGI::Request->multipart_stream: pull-based streaming multipart parsing with
    app-controlled sinks; mutually exclusive with the buffered body methods.
  - The standalone Redirect and NotFound applications build a PAGI::Response
    value (modules kept for the dynamic case).
  - PAGI::Lifespan coerces its app arg via to_app, and reports shutdown-handler
    failures instead of swallowing them.
  - Spec: URLMap sets root_path; WrapCGI builds SCRIPT_NAME from root_path.
  - Canonical scope-adding middleware route through the modify_scope helper.
  - Documented the PAGI::Response subclassing seam for framework authors.
  - Docs: recipes are now PAGI::Tools::Cookbook; Tutorial/Cookbook examples
    corrected against the current API; chat-showcase example uses PAGI::App::Router
    + PAGI::Lifespan.
  - For changes prior to 0.002000, see the Changes file of the PAGI distribution
    (versions up to 0.001023).
